1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,870
This is the build tutorial for the bubble clown costume. Make a gun shape with either hand,

2
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:09,450
squeeze, and a comet of light runs from your elbow down to your fingertips,

3
00:00:09,510 --> 00:00:14,030
arriving exactly as bubbles start firing out of your fingers. We will build it in twelve steps.

4
00:00:14,090 --> 00:00:18,610
Follow them in order and you will not have to guess at anything.

5
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:24,732
Two arms, two identical rigs. Each hand has its own trigger and fires its own arm.

6
00:00:24,792 --> 00:00:29,605
The light strips hide under the glove and the sleeve, so the glow reads as coming from inside the

7
00:00:29,665 --> 00:00:34,477
costume. And each arm comes apart into four modules that unplug from each other.

8
00:00:35,787 --> 00:00:40,807
Here is the sequence you are building. You squeeze. The blower gets a brief over drive pulse to break

9
00:00:40,867 --> 00:00:45,887
static friction, then settles to its run speed. A comet of light launches from the elbow.

10
00:00:45,947 --> 00:00:50,966
About two hundred and fifty milliseconds later the comet lands at your fingertips just as the blower

11
00:00:51,026 --> 00:00:56,046
reaches full speed, and bubbles start. The light travel is the spin up delay.

12
00:00:56,106 --> 00:01:01,126
That is the whole trick. It reads as the light causing the bubbles, instead of reading as lag.

13
00:01:02,435 --> 00:01:07,299
Nothing is soldered end to end across a joint. The pod sits on your upper arm and holds the brain

14
00:01:07,359 --> 00:01:12,224
and the battery. The sleeve holds the forearm strip. The glove holds the hand strip and that hand's

15
00:01:12,284 --> 00:01:17,148
trigger. And the bubbler is its own module. Each one unplugs, which is what lets you get out of the

16
00:01:17,208 --> 00:01:22,072
costume alone, with soapy hands, without dislocating a shoulder.

17
00:01:23,382 --> 00:01:28,635
There are five kinds of connector, and this is deliberate. No two plugs on one arm share both family

18
00:01:28,695 --> 00:01:33,948
and pin count. The three two pin plugs are three different pitches, so none of them will mate with

19
00:01:34,008 --> 00:01:39,261
the others. That is what stops you putting three point seven volts onto a logic pin in a dark room.

20
00:01:40,571 --> 00:01:45,793
This is the one rule you will use constantly. Battery out before you mate or unmate anything.

21
00:01:45,853 --> 00:01:51,076
Feeding data into an unpowered strip pushes current through its input protection diodes,

22
00:01:51,136 --> 00:01:56,358
and that is the classic way to kill pixel zero. Unplug the cell first. Every single time.

23
00:01:57,668 --> 00:02:02,285
Before you pick up the iron. Use the protected cells that came with the kits,

24
00:02:02,345 --> 00:02:06,962
and never charge a damaged or puffy one. It is strapped to your arm. Check polarity with a multimeter

25
00:02:07,022 --> 00:02:11,639
before you connect the battery the first time, because reversed power destroys the brain and the strip

26
00:02:11,699 --> 00:02:16,316
instantly and permanently. And solder somewhere ventilated.

27
00:02:17,626 --> 00:02:21,602
Steps one through ten build one complete arm. Step eleven is the second arm,

28
00:02:21,662 --> 00:02:25,638
which takes about half as long because you will already know what you are doing.

29
00:02:25,698 --> 00:02:29,674
Step twelve is the costume itself. One thing worth hearing now, before you start.

30
00:02:29,734 --> 00:02:33,710
Heat shrink goes onto the wire before you solder the joint, never after. Everybody learns this the

31
00:02:33,770 --> 00:02:37,746
hard way once.

32
00:02:39,056 --> 00:02:41,426
Step one. Measure your arm.

33
00:02:42,736 --> 00:02:47,222
Put on the glove and the sleeve you are actually going to use, and hold your arm out in the finger

34
00:02:47,282 --> 00:02:51,768
gun pose. Measure elbow to wrist crease, which is usually around twenty five centimetres.

35
00:02:51,828 --> 00:02:56,314
Then measure wrist crease to knuckles across the back of your hand, usually around ten.

36
00:02:56,374 --> 00:03:00,860
And mark the spot on your palm where your middle and ring finger pads land when you squeeze.

37
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:05,406
That is where the trigger goes. Use a fabric pen.

38
00:03:06,717 --> 00:03:11,720
Now turn those into pixel counts. At sixty LEDs per metre each pixel is one point six seven centimetres.

39
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:16,783
Divide each measurement by that. For typical arms you get about fifteen pixels on the forearm and

40
00:03:16,843 --> 00:03:21,846
six on the hand. Round down. A slightly short strip is fine. One that does not fit is not.

41
00:03:21,906 --> 00:03:26,909
Then subtract about four centimetres from the forearm figure, because the wrist umbilical and its

42
00:03:26,969 --> 00:03:31,972
connector need to live in the last few centimetres of forearm. Write these numbers down.

43
00:03:32,032 --> 00:03:37,035
You will type them into the firmware in step eight.

44
00:03:38,345 --> 00:03:40,471
Step two. Cut the strip.

45
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:47,097
The strip has marked cut lines between every pixel, with copper pads either side.

46
00:03:47,157 --> 00:03:52,472
Cut through the middle of the pads, not next to them, so you have pad left on both pieces to solder

47
00:03:52,532 --> 00:03:57,848
to. Cut two pieces, one for the forearm and one for the hand. Then check the little printed arrows.

48
00:03:57,908 --> 00:04:03,223
Data flows elbow to fingertips, so both pieces must have their arrows pointing the same way,

49
00:04:03,283 --> 00:04:08,599
toward your fingers. Tape the elbow end of the forearm piece. That end is pixel zero.

50
00:04:09,909 --> 00:04:13,784
It is worth saying plainly. Getting the arrows backwards is the single most common mistake in this

51
00:04:13,844 --> 00:04:17,719
build, and the fix is unsoldering everything. Check both pieces twice before the iron goes anywhere

52
00:04:17,779 --> 00:04:21,654
near them.

53
00:04:22,964 --> 00:04:27,758
Here is the layout you are aiming for. Fifteen pixels on the forearm, a gap of about eight centimetres

54
00:04:27,818 --> 00:04:32,613
at the wrist, then six pixels across the back of the hand. The reason it is split is that your wrist

55
00:04:32,673 --> 00:04:37,467
bends constantly. A single continuous strip across that joint will crack its internal copper traces

56
00:04:37,527 --> 00:04:42,322
within a few hours of wear, and everything past the crack goes dark. The flexible umbilical you build

57
00:04:42,382 --> 00:04:47,176
next absorbs that movement instead.

58
00:04:48,486 --> 00:04:51,112
Step three. Build the wrist umbilical.

59
00:04:52,422 --> 00:04:57,723
The umbilical carries five conductors. The strip's five volts, ground and data,

60
00:04:57,783 --> 00:05:03,083
plus this hand's two trigger wires. Use a J S T S M five pin pigtail pair, and fix this pin convention

61
00:05:03,143 --> 00:05:08,444
now. Pin one five volts, pin two ground, pin three data, pins four and five the trigger.

62
00:05:08,504 --> 00:05:13,804
Keep it identical on both arms, or your spares are not spares.

63
00:05:15,114 --> 00:05:20,012
Sleeve side first. Slide the heat shrink onto every wire now. Solder short silicone leads to the output

64
00:05:20,072 --> 00:05:24,970
end of the forearm piece, five volts, ground and data out, and take those three to pins one,

65
00:05:25,030 --> 00:05:29,928
two and three of one half of the pigtail. Pins four and five get two lengths of thin silicone wire,

66
00:05:29,988 --> 00:05:34,886
long enough to run all the way up the forearm to the elbow. That is the trigger's path to the pod.

67
00:05:34,946 --> 00:05:39,844
Shrink everything down, and seal the exposed strip end with a blob of hot glue or clear silicone.

68
00:05:39,904 --> 00:05:44,802
That is your soap proofing.

69
00:05:46,111 --> 00:05:51,042
Glove side. Solder pins one, two and three of the other pigtail half to the input end of the hand

70
00:05:51,102 --> 00:05:56,034
piece. Five volts to five volts, ground to ground, and pin three to data in.

71
00:05:56,094 --> 00:06:01,025
Data in, not data out. Pins four and five get wire long enough to reach your palm,

72
00:06:01,085 --> 00:06:06,017
ending in the female half of a J S T Z H two pin pigtail. That is where the microswitch will plug

73
00:06:06,077 --> 00:06:11,008
in at step five. Shrink and seal, same as before.

74
00:06:12,319 --> 00:06:17,408
Mate the pair about three to four centimetres above the wrist crease, on the forearm,

75
00:06:17,468 --> 00:06:22,556
where the skin barely moves. The crease itself is the worst possible place for a rigid twenty millimetre

76
00:06:22,616 --> 00:06:27,705
plastic body. The glove side wire is the flex element, so leave it long enough to cross the wrist

77
00:06:27,765 --> 00:06:32,854
with a service loop. Keep the whole thing short. Every centimetre between the last forearm pixel and

78
00:06:32,914 --> 00:06:38,003
the first hand pixel is dark arm that the comet has to cross. Eight centimetres is fine.

79
00:06:38,063 --> 00:06:43,151
Fifteen will look like a gap. Measure the finished pixel to pixel distance and write it down.

80
00:06:43,211 --> 00:06:48,300
Then gently tug every joint. Find a weak one now, not inside a sewn glove.

81
00:06:49,610 --> 00:06:55,533
Step four. Build the controller pod. This is the longest step, so take it in pieces.

82
00:06:56,843 --> 00:07:02,096
Start with the shape of the power. One protected eighteen six fifty cell feeds two rails.

83
00:07:02,156 --> 00:07:07,409
The blower motor runs straight off the cell at its native three point seven volts,

84
00:07:07,469 --> 00:07:12,723
which is exactly how the kit was designed. Only the strip and the brain get boosted to five volts,

85
00:07:12,783 --> 00:07:18,036
and that is a light load, about zero point three amps, so a small inexpensive boost module is genuinely

86
00:07:18,096 --> 00:07:23,349
adequate. A two amp polyfuse goes inline at the battery positive, before anything else.

87
00:07:24,659 --> 00:07:28,952
These are the core connections. The fuse goes first, right at the battery. Everything downstream of

88
00:07:29,012 --> 00:07:33,306
it shares one common ground. Data leaves pin D ten, through the level shifter,

89
00:07:33,366 --> 00:07:37,659
through a three hundred and thirty to four hundred and seventy ohm resistor,

90
00:07:37,719 --> 00:07:42,013
and out of the elbow connector. The trigger arrives on D one. Motor P W M leaves on D two.

91
00:07:42,073 --> 00:07:46,366
And D zero reads the battery.

92
00:07:47,676 --> 00:07:53,017
One detail that is easy to get wrong. The X I A O's pins only swing to three point three volts,

93
00:07:53,077 --> 00:07:58,417
but most so called logic level MOSFETs are specified fully on at five. At three point three they only

94
00:07:58,477 --> 00:08:03,818
partly open, they dissipate the difference as heat, and the blower runs slow and inconsistent.

95
00:08:03,878 --> 00:08:09,218
The level shifter in your pod has four gates and the strip only uses one. So route D two through a

96
00:08:09,278 --> 00:08:14,619
second gate, exactly the way you route the LED data through the first. The MOSFET then sees a clean

97
00:08:14,679 --> 00:08:20,019
five volt signal, and it costs you nothing. Add a hundred ohm resistor in series with the gate,

98
00:08:20,079 --> 00:08:25,420
and a ten k pulldown from gate to source, so the FET is held off while the board boots.

99
00:08:26,730 --> 00:08:31,493
And a warning that will save you an evening. Do not use an I R F five twenty module.

100
00:08:31,553 --> 00:08:36,317
It is the top search result for arduino mosfet module and it is not a logic level part.

101
00:08:36,376 --> 00:08:41,140
Its gate threshold runs up to four volts and it wants about ten to open properly.

102
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:45,963
It half works, which is much harder to diagnose than not working at all.

103
00:08:47,273 --> 00:08:51,571
Everything the pod sends down the arm leaves through exactly two plugs. A six pin at the elbow for

104
00:08:51,631 --> 00:08:55,930
the strip and the trigger, and a two pin for the motor. Pin six is not a spare,

105
00:08:55,990 --> 00:09:00,288
it is a job. The elbow run is the longest in the costume and carries the whole strip's current.

106
00:09:00,348 --> 00:09:04,646
A second ground conductor cuts the voltage drop and gives the data line a better return path.

107
00:09:04,706 --> 00:09:09,004
Tie it to common ground at both ends. And the motor gets its own plug on purpose.

108
00:09:09,064 --> 00:09:13,363
Roughly an amp of switched P W M bundled against a data line is asking for flicker.

109
00:09:13,423 --> 00:09:17,721
Twist the motor pair.

110
00:09:19,031 --> 00:09:24,080
The battery monitor is three connections. Two one hundred k resistors in series across the cell,

111
00:09:24,139 --> 00:09:29,188
and the midpoint goes to D zero. That midpoint sits at exactly half the battery voltage,

112
00:09:29,248 --> 00:09:34,297
which is safely inside what the brain can measure. The firmware uses it to pulse the elbow pixel red

113
00:09:34,356 --> 00:09:39,405
below three point four volts, so you get a warning instead of a sudden death.

114
00:09:40,715 --> 00:09:45,612
Two parts here are easy to fit backwards. The one thousand microfarad capacitor goes across the strip's

115
00:09:45,672 --> 00:09:50,570
five volts and ground, physically close to the elbow connector. Watch its polarity.

116
00:09:50,630 --> 00:09:55,527
The marked stripe is the negative leg. The one N five eight one nine diode goes directly across the

117
00:09:55,587 --> 00:10:00,485
motor's two terminals at the blower end, and its banded end goes to the positive side.

118
00:10:00,545 --> 00:10:05,442
Backwards, it is a dead short. Check that one twice.

119
00:10:06,752 --> 00:10:12,006
Four rules worth taping inside the pod. Everything shares one common ground.

120
00:10:12,066 --> 00:10:17,319
Skipping this causes bizarre, hard to diagnose behaviour. Keep the cell on its factory P H two plug,

121
00:10:17,379 --> 00:10:22,633
because the kit's charger mates to the same connector, so a flat cell goes pod to charger with no

122
00:10:22,693 --> 00:10:27,947
adapter. Never put anything on D eight or D nine, they are boot strapping pins.

123
00:10:28,007 --> 00:10:33,260
And design the printed pod so you can reach the U S B C port without taking anything apart.

124
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:38,574
Use a data capable cable. A charge only cable looks identical and will cost you half an hour.

125
00:10:39,884 --> 00:10:42,672
Step five. Wire this hand's trigger.

126
00:10:43,982 --> 00:10:48,425
Solder two lengths of thin silicone wire to the microswitch's common and normally open terminals,

127
00:10:48,485 --> 00:10:52,929
and terminate them in the male half of a Z H two pin pigtail. That is the mate to the one you left

128
00:10:52,989 --> 00:10:57,432
on the glove side in step three. Mount the switch on a small printed plate,

129
00:10:57,492 --> 00:11:01,936
positioned so the lever sits under your middle and ring finger pads, at the spot you marked in step

130
00:11:01,996 --> 00:11:06,439
one. Orient the lever so a natural squeeze presses across its length. You should be able to fire it

131
00:11:06,499 --> 00:11:10,943
with your eyes closed, in one motion, every time. If you find yourself aiming,

132
00:11:11,003 --> 00:11:15,446
move the plate.

133
00:11:16,756 --> 00:11:21,862
Two reasons this tiny plug exists. First, your field kit carries a spare microswitch already on a

134
00:11:21,922 --> 00:11:27,029
pigtail, so swapping a dead trigger at an event is a ten second job instead of a soldering job.

135
00:11:27,089 --> 00:11:32,195
Second, and more important, it must not be P H two point zero. Every cell in this build ships on a

136
00:11:32,255 --> 00:11:37,362
P H two lead. If the trigger used the same connector, one wrong plug in a dark room puts three point

137
00:11:37,422 --> 00:11:42,528
seven volts directly onto a logic pin. Z H is one point five millimetre pitch,

138
00:11:42,588 --> 00:11:47,695
P H is two. They will not mate. That incompatibility is the entire reason for the choice,

139
00:11:47,755 --> 00:11:52,861
so do not simplify it away later.

140
00:11:54,171 --> 00:11:58,399
Step six. Bench test, before anything goes into a glove.

141
00:11:59,709 --> 00:12:04,746
Lay the whole thing out flat on the table, fully wired, every connector mated,

142
00:12:04,806 --> 00:12:09,844
nothing mounted yet. Meter first. Battery plus and minus where you expect them,

143
00:12:09,904 --> 00:12:14,941
and the boost output reading close to five point zero volts. Only then connect the brain.

144
00:12:15,001 --> 00:12:20,038
Then beep through every connector, pin by pin. A crimp that did not seat looks perfect and works intermittently.

145
00:12:20,098 --> 00:12:25,135
Find that now, not at the venue. Power it up. You should see the dim breathing idle glow.

146
00:12:25,195 --> 00:12:30,233
Press the microswitch by hand. The comet should launch from the elbow end and travel toward the fingertips,

147
00:12:30,293 --> 00:12:35,330
and the motor should spin. Let it run five minutes and check for heat.

148
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:41,697
If the comet runs backwards, from the fingertips to the elbow, your strip pieces are reversed.

149
00:12:41,757 --> 00:12:46,815
Fix that in the wiring, not in the code. Both arms run byte identical firmware and keeping that true

150
00:12:46,875 --> 00:12:51,933
is worth the resolder. On heat, warm is fine, too hot to touch is not. Power down and look for a short.

151
00:12:51,992 --> 00:12:57,050
If you can borrow a thermal camera, use it here instead of your fingertip. It shows you a boost module

152
00:12:57,110 --> 00:13:02,168
running hot because something downstream is shorted, or a MOSFET that turned out not to be logic level.

153
00:13:02,227 --> 00:13:07,285
One borrowed session is plenty.

154
00:13:08,596 --> 00:13:10,931
Step seven. Fit the bubbler.

155
00:13:12,241 --> 00:13:17,495
First, understand what you actually have. The kit is not one object. It is a bottle,

156
00:13:17,555 --> 00:13:22,808
a cap with a one way valve, and a blower head, joined by silicone hose. There is about fifty millimetres

157
00:13:22,868 --> 00:13:28,122
of hose inside the bottle ending in a blue gravity ball, which sinks, so the pickup stays in solution

158
00:13:28,182 --> 00:13:33,436
at any arm angle, even inverted. Which means the heavy thing, the bottle, and the thing that must

159
00:13:33,496 --> 00:13:38,749
point past your fingertips, the blower head, do not have to be in the same place.

160
00:13:38,809 --> 00:13:44,063
That opens up a much better mount, if the blower can pull solution far enough.

161
00:13:45,374 --> 00:13:50,312
Now the hose test. Twenty minutes, one length of three by five silicone tube,

162
00:13:50,372 --> 00:13:55,310
and it decides your whole mount design. Do it the day the kit arrives, before any of the soldering.

163
00:13:55,370 --> 00:14:00,309
Assemble the kit as supplied and confirm it makes bubbles. Then replace the outside hose with about

164
00:14:00,369 --> 00:14:05,307
a hundred and fifty millimetres of tube, and run it again, held roughly the way your arm will hold

165
00:14:05,367 --> 00:14:10,305
it. The question is simply, does it still make bubbles at the same rate.

166
00:14:11,615 --> 00:14:15,838
If it passes, put the bottle on the forearm and the blower head at the knuckles,

167
00:14:15,898 --> 00:14:20,121
pointing past your fingertips. The bubbles still appear to come from your fingers,

168
00:14:20,182 --> 00:14:24,405
so the illusion is intact, and the heaviest item on the arm moves off the end of the lever where it

169
00:14:24,465 --> 00:14:28,688
was costing you the most. This is the better build if you can get it.

170
00:14:29,998 --> 00:14:34,391
If it fails, put both on the back of the hand, exactly as the kit intends, nozzle past the fingertips.

171
00:14:34,451 --> 00:14:38,844
It works as shipped with no modification and no risk. You just manage the weight instead.

172
00:14:38,904 --> 00:14:43,297
Run the bottle half full, mount the body back toward the wrist with the cap forward to shorten the

173
00:14:43,357 --> 00:14:47,750
lever arm, and anchor it to a wrist strap. A glove will not hold two hundred grams swinging for an

174
00:14:47,810 --> 00:14:52,203
evening.

175
00:14:53,513 --> 00:14:58,624
One job on the bubbler that is not cosmetic. The kit ships the motor on a P H two point zero female

176
00:14:58,684 --> 00:15:03,795
plug that mates straight to the cell. Cut it off and fit an S M two pin instead.

177
00:15:03,855 --> 00:15:08,966
If you leave it, the cell can be plugged directly into the motor, bypassing the MOSFET entirely,

178
00:15:09,026 --> 00:15:14,136
and then the trigger does nothing. While you are there, use silicone wire for the motor run,

179
00:15:14,196 --> 00:15:19,307
because it flexes constantly, and give the bubbler a quick release strap. Nothing about its mount

180
00:15:19,367 --> 00:15:24,478
should bridge the wrist, or the glove cannot come off without removing the bottle first.

181
00:15:25,788 --> 00:15:28,251
Step eight. Flash the firmware.

182
00:15:29,561 --> 00:15:34,514
Install the Arduino I D E, then add E S P thirty two board support through Boards Manager,

183
00:15:34,574 --> 00:15:39,526
searching for esp thirty two and installing the Espressif package. Then install the Fast L E D library

184
00:15:39,586 --> 00:15:44,539
from Library Manager. Select the X I A O E S P thirty two C three as your board,

185
00:15:44,599 --> 00:15:49,551
open the sketch, plug in U S B C and upload. If the upload fails, hold the boot button while plugging

186
00:15:49,611 --> 00:15:54,564
the cable in, to force bootloader mode.

187
00:15:55,874 --> 00:16:01,110
Before you upload, edit the three layout constants at the top of the sketch to match your own measurements.

188
00:16:01,170 --> 00:16:06,407
Forearm pixels, hand pixels, and gap pixels. Gap pixels is the measured pixel to pixel distance across

189
00:16:06,467 --> 00:16:11,703
the finished umbilical, connector body included, which is most of it, divided by one point six seven

190
00:16:11,763 --> 00:16:17,000
centimetres. About eight centimetres of umbilical gives you five. Guessing at this number is exactly

191
00:16:17,060 --> 00:16:22,296
what makes the comet look like it stumbles at the wrist. And flash the identical sketch to both arms.

192
00:16:22,356 --> 00:16:27,593
There is deliberately no left or right setting. As long as pixel zero is at the elbow on each arm,

193
00:16:27,653 --> 00:16:32,889
the mirroring is physical only.

194
00:16:34,199 --> 00:16:37,879
Step nine. Tune the timing. This is the step that makes the whole effect work,

195
00:16:37,939 --> 00:16:41,619
and it can only be done on the finished arm.

196
00:16:42,929 --> 00:16:48,204
The goal is that the comet reaches your fingertips at the exact moment the first bubble appears.

197
00:16:48,264 --> 00:16:53,539
When it is right, the light looks like it is causing the bubbles. When it is wrong by even a tenth

198
00:16:53,599 --> 00:16:58,874
of a second, it looks like lag. So film yourself firing it in slow motion. Any modern phone does a

199
00:16:58,934 --> 00:17:04,208
hundred and twenty or two hundred and forty frames a second. Watch it back frame by frame.

200
00:17:04,268 --> 00:17:09,543
If the comet arrives before the bubbles, increase comet travel milliseconds to slow it down.

201
00:17:09,603 --> 00:17:14,878
If it arrives after, decrease it. Reflash, film again. Expect three or four rounds.

202
00:17:14,938 --> 00:17:20,213
The default is two hundred and fifty milliseconds, and if you built the forearm bottle mount,

203
00:17:20,273 --> 00:17:25,548
expect a slightly longer number, because a longer feed hose takes marginally longer to prime.

204
00:17:26,858 --> 00:17:29,936
Step ten. Mount it into the glove and the sleeve.

205
00:17:31,246 --> 00:17:36,458
Hiding the strip under fabric is a genuine upgrade, not a compromise, because the fabric diffuses

206
00:17:36,518 --> 00:17:41,730
the pixels into one smooth continuous streak instead of visible dots. But it only works if the fabric

207
00:17:41,790 --> 00:17:47,003
cooperates. Thin, pale and stretchy glows beautifully. Thick or dark, especially leather,

208
00:17:47,062 --> 00:17:52,275
swallows almost all of it. So before you commit, hold a powered section of strip under the actual

209
00:17:52,335 --> 00:17:57,547
glove, in a dark room. If you cannot see it clearly, no firmware setting will rescue it.

210
00:17:57,607 --> 00:18:02,819
Get a different glove. And test the sleeve at the same time.

211
00:18:04,130 --> 00:18:08,816
If you are building the lace version from the design board, this section is overridden.

212
00:18:08,876 --> 00:18:13,563
Lace is open mesh. Light passes straight through the holes, so the strip stays visibly a strip,

213
00:18:13,623 --> 00:18:18,309
a hard bright line with dots, rather than a glow. You can lean into that, because circuitry under

214
00:18:18,369 --> 00:18:23,056
something delicate is on theme. Or you can add a diffuser layer underneath,

215
00:18:23,116 --> 00:18:27,802
thin white organza, tulle, or a frosted silicone channel, which gives you the soft glow with the lace

216
00:18:27,862 --> 00:18:32,549
pattern silhouetted on top. It is a taste call and it is cheap to test both.

217
00:18:32,609 --> 00:18:37,295
Do it with a scrap, before the gloves are cut into.

218
00:18:38,604 --> 00:18:42,982
Route the strip across the back of the hand, over the knuckles. Never the palm.

219
00:18:43,042 --> 00:18:47,421
That is where the trigger is and where you grip things. Stop short of the fingertips,

220
00:18:47,481 --> 00:18:51,859
because the nozzle needs that space and finger joints flex too much for strip to survive.

221
00:18:51,919 --> 00:18:56,297
Sew a fabric channel for the strip to sit in rather than gluing it down flat,

222
00:18:56,357 --> 00:19:00,736
so it can slide a little as your hand flexes. Glued taut strip tears itself off,

223
00:19:00,796 --> 00:19:05,174
or tears the glove. And the connector is never the anchor. Tack the cable to the garment on both sides

224
00:19:05,234 --> 00:19:09,612
of every plug, so a snag pulls the tack rather than the latch. Leave a service loop either side.

225
00:19:09,672 --> 00:19:14,051
Finally, a smear of dielectric grease in each connector shell keeps soap residue from corroding the

226
00:19:14,111 --> 00:19:18,489
contacts.

227
00:19:19,800 --> 00:19:23,254
Step eleven. Build the second arm. It is a repeat of steps one to ten, with two things to be careful

228
00:19:23,314 --> 00:19:26,768
about.

229
00:19:28,077 --> 00:19:33,471
Pixel zero goes at the elbow on this arm too. It is tempting to mirror the wiring along with the physical

230
00:19:33,531 --> 00:19:38,925
build. Do not. Identical wiring means identical firmware, which means one thing to maintain instead

231
00:19:38,985 --> 00:19:44,378
of two. And keep the same five pin and six pin conventions. If one arm puts the trigger on pins four

232
00:19:44,438 --> 00:19:49,832
and five and the other puts it on one and two, then your spares are not spares,

233
00:19:49,892 --> 00:19:55,286
and one wrong plug at an event costs you a board. Write the pinout on a bit of tape inside each pod.

234
00:19:56,596 --> 00:19:59,314
Step twelve. Assemble the costume.

235
00:20:00,625 --> 00:20:05,280
With both arms built, run a checkout before any event. Cells out of both pods.

236
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:09,996
Mate every connector and count them, four per arm, plus the Z H two at each microswitch.

237
00:20:10,056 --> 00:20:14,711
Cells in, and both arms should show the idle glow. Fire each hand separately.

238
00:20:14,771 --> 00:20:19,427
The left trigger drives the left arm only, the right drives the right only.

239
00:20:19,486 --> 00:20:24,142
If one trigger fires the other arm, you have crossed something, because there is no cross arm wiring

240
00:20:24,202 --> 00:20:28,857
in this design at all. Then fire both at once and watch for either arm dimming or glitching.

241
00:20:28,917 --> 00:20:33,573
They are electrically independent, so they should not interact. Then run both for the length of a

242
00:20:33,633 --> 00:20:38,288
bottle of solution. That is your real duty cycle.

243
00:20:39,598 --> 00:20:44,981
Getting into it. Order matters, and cells go in last, always. Pods onto the upper arms and strapped,

244
00:20:45,041 --> 00:20:50,424
with the cells out. Sleeves on, and mate the elbow six pin and two pin on each side.

245
00:20:50,484 --> 00:20:55,867
Gloves on, and mate the wrist five pin. Bubblers strapped on, mate the wrist two pin,

246
00:20:55,927 --> 00:21:01,310
bottles filled. Then cells in, and check both idle glows before you walk out the door.

247
00:21:02,620 --> 00:21:07,156
Getting out is the reverse, and it is the whole point of the build. Cells out first.

248
00:21:07,216 --> 00:21:11,752
Both arms are now dead and safe to unplug. Wrist two pin and the bubbler strap,

249
00:21:11,812 --> 00:21:16,348
and the bottles go somewhere upright. Wrist five pin, and the gloves peel off.

250
00:21:16,408 --> 00:21:20,944
Elbow six pin and two pin, and the sleeves come off. Then the pod straps. Four releases per arm,

251
00:21:21,004 --> 00:21:25,540
and none of them need a second person or a flat surface.

252
00:21:26,850 --> 00:21:31,835
Here is the test that matters. Can you get out of it alone, in a bathroom, with soapy hands,

253
00:21:31,895 --> 00:21:36,881
in under a minute? If you cannot, then something is still soldered that should not be,

254
00:21:36,941 --> 00:21:41,926
or a strap is fighting a connector. Fix that before the event, not at it.

255
00:21:43,236 --> 00:21:48,310
Afterwards. Cells out for storage, always. Never store the costume with cells connected.

256
00:21:48,370 --> 00:21:53,444
Rinse the blower head and the cap in warm water, because dried bubble solution glues the one way valve

257
00:21:53,504 --> 00:21:58,577
shut. Leave the silicone sleeve on the cap protrusion so the bottle does not empty into your bag.

258
00:21:58,637 --> 00:22:03,711
And wipe soap residue off any connector that got sprayed, then re grease it.

259
00:22:05,021 --> 00:22:09,720
Most faults in this build come from a short list. If nothing lights at all,

260
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:14,479
check your common ground, your boost output, and that data is going into the input end of the strip.

261
00:22:14,539 --> 00:22:19,238
If it worked yesterday and not today, check every latch, because a half seated plug looks mated.

262
00:22:19,298 --> 00:22:23,997
If pixel zero died after a reconnect, the strip was plugged in live. If the comet jumps at the wrist,

263
00:22:24,057 --> 00:22:28,756
your gap pixels number does not match reality. And if the motor is weak and the MOSFET is hot,

264
00:22:28,816 --> 00:22:33,515
your gate is being driven at three point three volts, or the part is not logic level.

265
00:22:33,575 --> 00:22:38,274
The full table is in the build manual.

266
00:22:39,584 --> 00:22:44,167
Last thing. Your field kit. Carry a charged spare cell per arm, a pre made spare wrist umbilical,

267
00:22:44,227 --> 00:22:48,809
a spare microswitch already on its pigtail, and one spare S M pigtail pair of each size.

268
00:22:48,869 --> 00:22:53,452
Those are the things that fail during an event, and because of the connector design,

269
00:22:53,512 --> 00:22:58,094
all of them now swap without a soldering iron. Also carry extra bubble solution.

270
00:22:58,154 --> 00:23:02,737
It runs out long before the battery does.

271
00:23:04,046 --> 00:23:08,580
And that is the build. Work one arm at a time, bench test before you sew anything into a glove,

272
00:23:08,640 --> 00:23:13,174
and keep the cell out of the pod whenever you are plugging things together.

273
00:23:13,234 --> 00:23:17,768
Full detail is in the build manual, the reasoning behind every part is in the parts list,

274
00:23:17,828 --> 00:23:22,362
and the costume direction is in the design document. Go make some bubbles.
